findings.body:
CONTRACT.md (untouched by this PR — `git diff 1581accd2691ec836fe60c97339d1600d931132f..HEAD -- templates/agents/hooks/CONTRACT.md` is empty), lines 44-47:

> "`git-safety` matches patterns against the raw command text. A command that merely *contains* a dangerous pattern as data — a heredoc writing documentation, a `grep` for the pattern itself — is blocked as a false positive. This is deliberate: the alternative is parsing shell..."

The PR's new test file directly contradicts this for exactly the cases CONTRACT.md names as examples:

```js
it.each([
  ["an echo", `echo "${PHRASE}"`],
  ["a grep pattern", `grep -rn "${PHRASE}" docs/`],
  ["a heredoc", `cat > x.md <<EOF\nto release, run ${PHRASE}\nEOF`],
  ...
])("allows %s that only names a forbidden push", (_label, command) => {
  expect(run(repoOn("feat/x"), command).status).toBe(0);
});
```
— https://github.com/AlexisBalayre/agentspine/blob/faeb529d6d0cf6f28e6e7c2cf3933620c7f32ecd/test/claude-code-adapter.test.ts#L186-L195

CONTRACT.md is the normalized contract every host adapter (Claude Code, opencode, Codex, Mistral Vibe, Cursor) is written against; leaving its 'Known limitation' section describing behavior the shipped code no longer has for most rules (only `rm -rf /` still does whole-string matching, per the PR's own stated, narrower carve-out) will mislead whoever next audits or extends an adapter against this contract.

